<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/morphoeval-delphi10000-dugesia-japonica-turing-wavelength-s0> a pk:Claim ;
    rdfs:label "Saturated Schnakenberg Turing wavelength for Dugesia japonica dorsal pigment at 6.912 µm (Holocene, Pass 1) (Dugesia japonica (freshwater planarian))",
        "Saturated Schnakenberg Turing wavelength for Dugesia japonica dorsal pigment at 6.912 µm (Holocene, Pass 1) faithfully reproduces Dugesia japonica (freshwater planarian) morphology" ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/delphi10000-dugesia-japonica-turing-wavelength-s0-1>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/delphi10000-dugesia-japonica-turing-wavelength-s0-2>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/delphi10000-dugesia-japonica-turing-wavelength-s0-3>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/delphi10000-dugesia-japonica-turing-wavelength-s0-4>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/delphi10000-dugesia-japonica-turing-wavelength-s0-5> ;
    dct:description "The simulation script(s) models/2026-delphi10000-dugesia-japonica-turing-wavelength-s0.py implement the cited source model as published and produce forms that match real Dugesia japonica (freshwater planarian) specimens and the source authors' figures. A belief under audit, not a fact: its credibility is carried by the MorphoEval rubric assessments." ;
    dct:subject <https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/main/models/2026-delphi10000-dugesia-japonica-turing-wavelength-s0.py> ;
    schema:about "Dugesia japonica (freshwater planarian)" ;
    schema:additionalProperty "does_not_claim: Does not claim a fossil predecessor. The chronoseries is Holocene 1/1, age 0 Ma.",
        "does_not_claim: Does not claim that 6.912 µm was measured on Dugesia japonica in any of the cited papers. It is this slot's detail-scale operating point.",
        "does_not_claim: Does not claim the rendered body outlines are a pixel-perfect copy of four separate worms; the brightness mask merges some animals.",
        "reconstructed_in_script: Body mask and ocellus cups segmented from the Nogi plate and used as the display domain.",
        "reconstructed_in_script: Spectral diffusion plus explicit reaction, seed 3213, and the linear-mode Pearson comparison.",
        "reconstructed_in_script: κ = 0.15 saturation, R = D_v/D_u = 48, and D_u solved so λ_c = 6.912 µm on a 48.384 µm patch (seven linear wavelengths).",
        "reported_in_source: Nogi, Zhang, Chan & Marchant (2009) published the CC BY 2.5 plate of regenerated Dugesia japonica with dorsal pigment and ocelli.",
        "reported_in_source: Orii, Ito & Watanabe (2002) describe extant Dugesia japonica anatomy. Herath & Lobo (2020) model planarian fission with Turing patterns, not this wavelength.",
        "reported_in_source: Schnakenberg (1979) gives the polynomial activator-substrate kinetics; Gierer & Meinhardt (1972) and Murray (1981) are the pigment-pattern reading.",
        "reported_in_source: Turing (1952) gives the diffusion-driven instability and the critical wavenumber from the reaction Jacobian and the two diffusivities.",
        "transfer_boundary: Fish pigment waves (Kondo 2009) and Schmidtea eye regeneration (Lapan & Reddien 2012) are not measurements of a 6.912 µm Dugesia japonica mode.",
        "transfer_boundary: The Nogi plate is a praziquantel-regeneration micrograph without a scale bar. Pixel size is not 6.912 µm." ;
    pk:anatomicalStructure "Turing pigment stripe / ocellus wavelength in Dugesia japonica" ;
    pk:aristotelianEfficientCause "1. Chemical Turing / RD" ;
    pk:aristotelianMegistonGenos "II. Anaima (Invertebrata)" ;
    pk:aristotelianPoionForm "Stripes, Spots & Ocelli" ;
    pk:aristotelianPosonLaw "Turing Wavenumber k_c" ;
    pk:aristotelianSubalternClass "Zoophyta & Vermes (Cnidaria/Platyhelminthes)" ;
    pk:biologicalGenus "Dugesia" ;
    pk:context "script(s): https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/main/models/2026-delphi10000-dugesia-japonica-turing-wavelength-s0.py; output: docs/media/dugesia_japonica_turing_wavelength_s0_comparison.png; organism: Dugesia japonica (freshwater planarian); environment: python 3.12, lean not installed in this VM; theorems are Lean 4 prelude, unchecked here; claim_boundary: [reported_in_source: Turing (1952) gives the diffusion-driven instability and the critical wavenumber from the reaction Jacobian and the two diffusivities. | Schnakenberg (1979) gives the polynomial activator-substrate kinetics; Gierer & Meinhardt (1972) and Murray (1981) are the pigment-pattern reading. | Nogi, Zhang, Chan & Marchant (2009) published the CC BY 2.5 plate of regenerated Dugesia japonica with dorsal pigment and ocelli. | Orii, Ito & Watanabe (2002) describe extant Dugesia japonica anatomy. Herath & Lobo (2020) model planarian fission with Turing patterns, not this wavelength.; reconstructed_in_script: κ = 0.15 saturation, R = D_v/D_u = 48, and D_u solved so λ_c = 6.912 µm on a 48.384 µm patch (seven linear wavelengths). | Spectral diffusion plus explicit reaction, seed 3213, and the linear-mode Pearson comparison. | Body mask and ocellus cups segmented from the Nogi plate and used as the display domain.; transfer_boundary: Fish pigment waves (Kondo 2009) and Schmidtea eye regeneration (Lapan & Reddien 2012) are not measurements of a 6.912 µm Dugesia japonica mode. | The Nogi plate is a praziquantel-regeneration micrograph without a scale bar. Pixel size is not 6.912 µm.; does_not_claim: Does not claim that 6.912 µm was measured on Dugesia japonica in any of the cited papers. It is this slot's detail-scale operating point. | Does not claim a fossil predecessor. The chronoseries is Holocene 1/1, age 0 Ma. | Does not claim the rendered body outlines are a pixel-perfect copy of four separate worms; the brightness mask merges some animals.]; phenomenon_size: 8.9 µm (8.928e-06 